      "title": "Learning Doc",
      "content": {
        "doc_md": "HTML DOM allows JavaScript to react to HTML events:\n\nOften, when events happen, you may want to do something.\n\nWhen JavaScript is used in HTML pages,JavaScript can react on events.\n\nJavaScript lets you execute code when events are detected.\n\nHTML allows event handler attributes,with JavaScript code, to be added to HTML elements.\n\nIn the following example, anonclickattribute (with code), is added to a<button>element:\n\nIn the example above, the JavaScript code changes the content of \nthe element with id=\"demo\".\n\nIn the next example, the code changes the content of \nits own element usingthis.innerHTML:\n\nJavaScript code can often be several lines long.\n\nIt is more common to use the event attribute to call a function:\n\nHere is a list of some common HTML events:\n\nThe list is much longer:W3Schools JavaScript Reference HTML DOM Events.\n\nAn event handler is JavaScript code that runs when an event happens.\n\nEvent handlers can be used to handle and verify user input, user actions, \nand browser actions:\n\nMany different methods can be used to let JavaScript work with events:\n\nUsing event attributes likeonclickare easy to use.\n\nNevertheless, usingaddEventListener()is the recommended way to handle events.\n\nTheaddEventListener()method keeps HTML and JavaScript separated.\n\n### Code Snippets\n\n```\n<\nelement\nevent\n=\n'\nsome JavaScript\n'\n>\n\n---\n\n<\nelement\nevent\n=\n\"\nsome JavaScript\n\"\n>\n\n---\n\n<button onclick=\"document.getElementById('demo').innerHTML = Date()\">The time is?</button>\n\n---\n\n<button onclick=\"this.innerHTML = Date()\">The time is?</button>\n\n---\n\n<button onclick=\"displayDate()\">The time is?</button>\n<script>\nfunction displayDate() {\ndocument.getElementById(\"demo\").innerHTML = Date();\n}\n</script>\n\n---\n\n<button onclick=\"displayDate()\">Time is?</button>\n<p id=\"demo\"></p>\n<script>\n// Function to display date\nfunction displayDate() {\ndocument.getElementById(\"demo\").innerHTML = Date();\n}\n</script>\n\n---\n\n<button id=\"myBtn\">Click me</button>\n<p id=\"demo\"></p>\n<script>\nconst btn = document.getElementById(\"myBtn\");\n// Add EventListener to btn\nbtn.addEventListener(\"click\", function () {\ndocument.getElementById(\"demo\").innerHTML = Date();\n});\n</script>\n```"
      }
    },
